1. OOP Concepts – Class, Object & Methods: To implement basic Object-Oriented Programming concepts in Java.

1.a) a) Create a class Student with data members and methods.

Aim
To create a Student class with data members and methods in Java.

PROGRAM :-

class Student {
    // Data members
    int rollNo;
    String name;
    int marks;
    // Method to assign values
    void getData() {
        rollNo = 101;
        name = "Rahul";
        marks = 85;
    }

    // Method to display values
    void displayData() {
        System.out.println("Roll No: " + rollNo);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Marks: " + marks);
    }
    // Main method
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        s.getData();
        s.displayData();
    }
}

OUTPUT :-

Roll No: 101
Name: Rahul
Marks: 85


b) Demonstrate Object Creation and Method Invocation

Aim
To demonstrate object creation and method invocation in Java.

PROGRAM :- 

class Student {
    // Data members
    int rollNo = 101;
    String name = "Rahul";
    // Method
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Roll No: " + rollNo);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Object creation
        Student s = new Student();
        // Method invocation
        s.display();
    }
}

OUTPUT :-

Roll No: 101
Name: Rahul 

c) Implement Constructor (Default and Parameterized)

Aim
To implement default and parameterized constructors in Java.

Program :- 

class Student {
    int rollNo;
    String name;

    // Default Constructor
    Student() {
        rollNo = 101;
        name = "Rahul";
    }

    // Parameterized Constructor
    Student(int r, String n) {
        rollNo = r;
        name = n;
    }

    // Method to display details
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Roll No: " + rollNo);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Using Default Constructor
        Student s1 = new Student();
        // Using Parameterized Constructor
        Student s2 = new Student(102, "Priya");
        s1.display();
        s2.display();
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Roll No: 101
Name: Rahul

Roll No: 102
Name: Priya

d) Demonstrate method overloading.
Aim
To demonstrate method overloading in Java.

PROGRAM :-

class Student {
    // Method with no parameters
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Student Name: Rahul");
    }
    // Overloaded method with one parameter
    void display(int marks) {
        System.out.println("Marks: " + marks);
    }
    // Overloaded method with two parameters
    void display(String name, int marks) {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Marks: " + marks);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        s.display();
        s.display(85);
        s.display("Priya", 90);
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Student Name: Rahul
Marks: 85
Name: Priya
Marks: 90


